Package: insectecol
Type: Package
Title: Insect Ecology Data Analysis Toolkit
Version: 1.1.0
Authors@R: person(given = "Wangyao", family = "Li", email = "1561941342@qq.com", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: A collection of analytical tools for insect ecology research,
    currently covering age-stage, two-sex life table analysis and
    dose-response bioassays. The life table module supports fast batch
    processing of multi-group datasets, validates raw 'csv' data, computes
    cohort size, mean fecundity, age-stage survival rates, age-specific
    survival, age-specific fecundity, life expectancy, and derived
    population parameters (net reproductive rate, intrinsic and finite
    rates of increase, mean generation time), simultaneously generates
    age-stage survival curves for all groups, and exports all tabular
    results and plots to 'Excel' in a single run. The bioassay module
    estimates lethal concentrations by the traditional and the weighted
    (improved) linear regression methods and by probit analysis, with
    Abbott correction, 95% confidence intervals and chi-square
    goodness-of-fit tests; the lethal proportion can be set freely (e.g.,
    25%, 50%, 70% or 90%), so any LC value such as the LC25, LC70 or
    LC90 can be computed, not only the LC50. The regression plots and
    tables are exported to 'Excel'. The degree-day module estimates the
    developmental threshold temperature and the effective accumulated
    temperature with the linear degree-day law, fits common nonlinear
    temperature-dependent development models (Logan-6, Lactin, Briere,
    Wang-7), selects the best model by AICc, predicts durations and
    accumulates field degree-days. The emergence module projects the beginning, peak and end of the
    adult emergence period (the 16%, 50% and 84% quantiles) from a
    single survey of the population stage structure by the stage-grading
    method, optionally shifting the eclosion dates by the
    pre-oviposition period and the egg duration to forecast larval
    hatch. Further extensions, such as median lethal temperature/time
    (LT50), are planned.
